## 4. Logger

### How It Works

NestJS has a built-in `Logger` class. Each service creates its own instance with a context name.

```typescript
import { Logger } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class MyService {
  private readonly logger = new Logger(MyService.name);  // Context = "MyService"

  async doSomething() {
    this.logger.log('Something happened');        // [MyService] Something happened
    this.logger.warn('Watch out');                 // [MyService] Watch out
    this.logger.error('Failed', error.stack);      // [MyService] Failed + stack trace
    this.logger.debug('Debug info');               // Only shown when debug mode is on
  }
}
```

### When to Use Each Level

| Level | When |
|-------|------|
| [log()](file:///d:/code/final/backend/src/modules/auth/auth.service.ts#14-62) | Normal operations: "Enquiry created", "Message sent" |
| [warn()](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/components/ui/Toast.tsx#73-75) | Not wrong but unusual: "Retry attempt 2/3", "Stale enquiry found" |
| [error()](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/components/ui/Toast.tsx#71-73) | Something failed: always include stack → `this.logger.error('msg', error.stack)` |
| `debug()` | Verbose — only during development |

### Best Practices

```typescript
// ✅ Good — includes context and IDs
this.logger.log(`📨 Enquiry ${enquiry.id} status changed: ${old} → ${newStatus}`);
this.logger.error(`Failed to send message for enquiry ${id}`, error.stack);

// ❌ Bad — no context, hard to trace
console.log("status changed");  // Don't use console.log — use the logger
```

> **Rule:** Replace all `console.log()` with `this.logger.log()`. The only exception is temporary debugging.

