## 5. Error Handling

### Backend: GlobalExceptionFilter

**File:** [global-exception.filter.ts](file:///d:/code/final/backend/src/common/filters/global-exception.filter.ts)

Catches everything and returns consistent error responses:

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "code": "NOT_FOUND",
    "message": "Record not found",
    "statusCode": 404
  },
  "timestamp": "2026-03-08T...",
  "path": "/api/v1/enquiry/abc123"
}
```

### What You Throw in Services

```typescript
// ── Common throws ──
throw new NotFoundException('Enquiry not found');           // 404
throw new BadRequestException('Invalid status transition'); // 400
throw new ForbiddenException("You can't do this");          // 403
throw new ConflictException('Version mismatch');            // 409
throw new UnauthorizedException();                          // 401

// ── Prisma errors are auto-mapped ──
// P2002 → 409 CONFLICT (duplicate)
// P2025 → 404 NOT_FOUND
// P2003 → 400 FOREIGN_KEY_ERROR

// ── Validation errors (from DTOs) ──
// class-validator errors → 400 with details array
```

### Frontend: API Client Error Classification

**File:** [api-client.ts](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/lib/api-client.ts)

The frontend [apiClient](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/lib/api-client.ts#121-256) catches every error and classifies it:

| HTTP Status | Error Type | What It Means |
|------------|------------|---------------|
| 0 (no response) | `NETWORK_ERROR` | Can't reach server |
| 400 | `VALIDATION_ERROR` | DTO validation failed |
| 401 | `AUTH_ERROR` | Token expired |
| 403 | `FORBIDDEN_ERROR` | CASL permission denied |
| 404 | `NOT_FOUND_ERROR` | Resource doesn't exist |
| 409 | `CONFLICT_ERROR` | Version mismatch / duplicate |
| 500+ | `SERVER_ERROR` | Backend crashed |

---

## 6. Response Envelope

### Backend Wraps Everything

[ResponseInterceptor](file:///d:/code/final/backend/src/common/interceptors/response.interceptor.ts) automatically wraps:

```typescript
// Your service returns:
return { id: '123', name: 'Rahul' };

// Client receives:
{
  "success": true,
  "data": { "id": "123", "name": "Rahul" },
  "timestamp": "2026-..."
}
```

### Frontend Unwraps Automatically

[apiClient](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/lib/api-client.ts#121-256) detects `{ success, data }` and returns just `data`:

```typescript
// In your service:
const enquiry = await apiClient<Enquiry>('/enquiry/123');
// enquiry = { id: '123', name: 'Rahul' }  ← Already unwrapped
```

---

## 7. Event System

### How Modules Communicate

Uses `@nestjs/event-emitter` — modules emit events, other modules listen.

```typescript
// ── Emitting (in any service) ──
import { EventEmitter2 } from '@nestjs/event-emitter';

this.eventEmitter.emit('enquiry.qualified', {
  inboundMessageId: message.id,
  contactId: message.contactId,
  // ...
});

// ── Listening (in any other service) ──
@OnEvent('enquiry.qualified')
async handleQualified(payload: QualifiedDto) {
  // Create enquiry from qualified inbound message
}
```

### Current Events in the System

| Event | Emitter | Listener | Purpose |
|-------|---------|----------|---------|
| `enquiry.qualified` | QualificationService | EnquiryService | Creates enquiry after AI qualifies a message |
| `message.outbound` | EnquiryService | OutboundService | Sends reply via WhatsApp/Email/SMS |

---

## 8. Job Queue (BullMQ)

### How Background Jobs Work

```typescript
// ── 1. Register queue in module ──
BullModule.registerQueue({ name: 'qualification' })

// ── 2. Add job from service ──
await this.qualificationQueue.add('qualify', { inboundMessageId: id }, {
  attempts: 3,
  backoff: { type: 'exponential', delay: 2000 },
});

// ── 3. Process in worker ──
@Processor('qualification')
export class QualificationWorker extends WorkerHost {
  async process(job: Job<{ inboundMessageId: string }>) {
    // AI classification logic here
  }
}
```

> **Requires:** Redis running on `REDIS_HOST:REDIS_PORT`

---

## 9. Frontend Architecture

### The Layered Flow

```
Pages (Server/Client Components)
  └── Services (Server Actions — 'use server')
       └── apiClient (lib/api-client.ts — HTTP + retry + logging)
            └── NestJS Backend
```

### Key Principle

> **apiClient** is the ONLY thing that makes HTTP calls.
> **Services** are the ONLY things that call apiClient.
> **Components** are the ONLY things that call services.

### Where Permissions Are Checked

| Layer | How | File |
|-------|-----|------|
| Server Components | [can(user.permissions, 'read', 'Enquiry')](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/lib/permissions.ts#81-96) | [lib/permissions.ts](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/lib/permissions.ts) |
| Client Components | `useAuthStore().can('read', 'Enquiry')` | [stores/auth-store.ts](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/stores/auth-store.ts) |
| Navigation | Filtered in [DashboardLayout](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/components/DashboardLayout.tsx#35-72) → from [lib/navigation.tsx](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/lib/navigation.tsx) | [lib/navigation.tsx](file:///d:/code/final/frontend/lib/navigation.tsx) |

### How the Zustand Store Gets Populated

```
1. User visits /dashboard
2. DashboardLayout (server component) calls getCurrentUser()
3. getCurrentUser() calls /auth/me → gets { user, permissions }
4. DashboardLayout renders <AuthHydrator user={user} permissions={permissions} />
5. AuthHydrator (client component) calls useAuthStore().setSession()
6. setSession() builds CASL ability from permission rules
7. Now any client component can do: useAuthStore().can('read', 'Enquiry')
```

